 "timeline": {
  "current_model": "PLD3 is a type II transmembrane lysosomal protein that functions as a 5'-to-3' single-stranded DNA and RNA exonuclease (degrading TLR7/TLR9 ligands to prevent autoinflammation), synthesizes the critical lysosomal phospholipid S,S-BMP via glycerol stereo-inversion, and undergoes sequential post-translational modifications (N-glycosylation, AMPylation by FICD, and proteolytic cleavage) that regulate its activation; its loss or disease-associated variants (e.g., V232M) impair nuclease and phospholipase activities, disrupt endolysosomal biogenesis in neurons, cause axonal spheroid accumulation with conduction blockade, and dysregulate innate immune nucleic-acid sensing through both endosomal TLR and STING pathways."
